THE TWO FLAGGED RISKS -- both settled with evidence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RISK 1 -- predicted MiM-cap top-plate short (route_nets.py flagged 2 DIRTY
corridor landings: "VDD on XC0 (top_met)" and "VDD on XC1 (top_met)", reproduced
by all five candidate placements).
  RESOLUTION: FALSE ALARM on this build. Evidence, not inference:
    a) The extracted top-level instantiates the caps on three DISTINCT nets:
         XXC0_0 voutp VDD XC0
         XXC1_0 voutn VDD XC1
    b) Net count is 7 on BOTH sides. A VDD/voutp or VDD/voutn merge would have
       shown 6 on the layout side.
    c) The comparison that would catch such a merge is provably live -- see
       negative control (b) below, which swaps exactly XC1's terminal from
       voutn to voutp and is correctly rejected.
  So the route_nets.py check fired structurally on the stacked-plate macro
  geometry, as it does on any such macro, and did not correspond to a real
  extraction-level short here. NO fix iterations were spent on it, and no
  mimcap.py change is required by THIS run's evidence. (If a future build does
  show the merge, the fix is a top-plate breakout to the macro edge in
  mimcap.py, mirroring fet.py::_stretch_terminals_to_edge() -- a cell defect,
  not a placement or routing knob.)

RISK 2 -- lvs_compare.sp carries no XL0/XL1 while the layout has two
vco_spiral_ind subcells.
  RESOLUTION: no delta appeared, and the mechanism is NOT 'ignore class'.
  Because the black-boxed vco_spiral_ind contains no devices, ext2spice omits
  BOTH the subcircuit definition AND its instance calls. Confirmed: grep for
  'spiral|XL0|XL1' in routed_extracted.spice returns nothing at all. The two
  sides therefore agree structurally with no netgen-side treatment applied.
  This is a netlist/extraction-view question, as flagged, not a layout defect.

